# Summary
Introduced: the commit expands the customer-facing answer/context from a single best chunk to all matching chunks and adds an LLM context builder, both without filtering out `agents_only` documents.
Knowledge documents support a visibility value of `agents_only`, but the inbound WhatsApp RAG path retrieves all active chunks for the tenant regardless of visibility. This commit makes the default template answer concatenate all matching chunk excerpts instead of only the best chunk, so a lower-ranked `agents_only` chunk can now be directly included in the outbound customer message. The new `local_llm` engine also builds LLM context from all matches without checking visibility, allowing the model to answer from internal-only material. An unauthenticated WhatsApp contact can ask a question with terms overlapping both public and internal documents and receive sensitive internal policy, pricing, discount, escalation, or operational content intended only for agents.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the data model has an internal-only visibility state (`agents_only`) distinct from customer-safe visibility.
- [x] Confirm the WhatsApp RAG retrieval query only filters tenant/status and does not exclude `agents_only` documents before ranking.
- [x] Confirm ranking preserves visibility but does not use it as an authorization/customer-safety filter.
- [x] Confirm template answer generation concatenates all sufficiently scored matches, allowing a lower-ranked `agents_only` chunk into the outbound answer.
- [x] Confirm local_llm context construction and /api/chat request include all sufficiently scored matches, including `agents_only` content.
## Report
Validated the information-disclosure finding with a targeted functional PoC and a non-interactive pdb trace. Code evidence: app/api/v1/schemas.py:183-193 allows visibility='agents_only'; app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:121-149 retrieves active tenant chunks with kd.status='active' but no kd.visibility filter before passing matches to template or LLM engines; app/services/rag_retrieval.py:111-137 preserves visibility but does not filter, and app/services/rag_retrieval.py:147-162 concatenates every match above min_score into the customer-facing template answer; app/services/llm_answer.py:28-37 builds LLM context from every match above min_score and app/services/llm_answer.py:62-75 sends that unfiltered context to /api/chat. The PoC created one tenant-visible chunk and one agents_only internal chunk with overlapping terms. Running `python /workspace/validation_artifacts/poc-agents-only-leak/poc_agents_only_leak.py` showed both chunks ranked: tenant score=0.89 and agents_only score=0.4981. The template answer included the internal string `SOLO_AGENTES: descuento VIP interno del 80% usando código SECRETO-AGENTES`. The fake Ollama server captured the real build_llm_answer /api/chat payload and the user message also contained the agents_only secret. The LLM decision returned an answered customer response containing `SECRETO-AGENTES`. A non-interactive pdb trace confirmed locals at app/services/rag_retrieval.py:160 contained both ('tenant', 0.89, ...) and ('agents_only', 0.4981, ...), and at app/services/llm_answer.py:62 the context variable contained the agents_only secret. Crash testing is not meaningful for this Python logic information disclosure; the faulthandler run exited 0 while reproducing the leak. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container and is not applicable to this Python authorization/filtering flaw. Relevant tests `pytest -q tests/test_rag_retrieval.py tests/test_answer_engine_static.py -q` passed.

